2026-10-24 T-317F: Capture normalized tool failures centrally in a machine-local SQLite ledger, with allowlisted summaries instead of raw payloads. Reuse authenticated API and Conductor tools for grouped triage and explicit remediation; never disable a tool from one failure. Keep diagnostics local because tool installations and runtime conditions differ per machine. Global prompt policy covers every role and harness; hidden inner calls require safe agent reporting. No live server restart or unsupported config edit. Source thread 317ffed2-e4bf-4748-812d-488b6dc0662a.
2026-10-28 T-TKOV: Take over a thread from another synced Mac as a request on top of Hand off: the asking Mac writes handoff.request.<thread>, the Mac that holds the thread answers with the usual offer, and the asking Mac takes it with the existing pickup. The holder between turns is handoff.holder.<thread>, written only by the Mac a thread moves to (a turn starting there, or a pickup), not a thread column, because the thread row is last-writer-wins and a rename on the other Mac would overwrite it. The holder answers only as holder and only onto a settled offer, so a stale Mac never gives an old session away; only the holder may Hand off. The asking Mac alone ends a request (cancel, one hour, or the thread moving), so no Mac compares clocks. A sleeping holder makes the request wait; forced takeover, checkpoints and crew threads stay out. Source threads 0cdcc478 (plan) and fdf85080-5a5d-427a-8f8f-aaa2cc390fe7.
2026-10-30 T-399: One execution owner per thread: an `assignments` row (machine, generation, routed) that every turn, pickup and routing claims, synced by generation so a late copy of an old owner never wins. The live registry derives from it; `run_machine` stays a status-write tag; `handoff.holder` and `runon.thread` are kept only for older builds. The check moved to the delivery boundary: `turnBlocked` for Ben's replies, `wakeBlocked` for Omni's own wakes (which never move a thread), applied in the runner's `wake`. Generation fencing stops a stale turn after sync. A disconnected machine may start work on what it holds and nothing else; no lease expires on its own. "Remote provider" (Hermes, Cursor Cloud: no local files) is named apart from "remote machine" (another synced Mac). Issue #399.
2026-10-31 T-410: One lifecycle owner per thread process: `runner/controller.ts` (ThreadController) is the only writer of a process's state; every other runner module reads it as `Live`, with each field read-only, and moves it through the controller's methods, so typecheck fails on a write elsewhere. The runner reaches run-on, handoff and publishing only through `runner/ports.ts`, bound once in `apps/server/compose.ts`, which also builds each controller's reporting, scheduling and persistence; `tests/import-boundaries.test.ts` fails on any runtime import cycle that crosses an owner. The states are drawn in docs/internals/execution-state.md. Issue #410.
2026-11-01 T-CONNECT: Omni Connect follows T3 Connect on the user's own relay, one user per relay. A machine signed in to sync registers with its relay token; the relay, given CF_API_TOKEN, CF_ACCOUNT_ID, CF_ZONE_ID and CONNECT_ZONE, provisions a Cloudflare tunnel and a proxied <name>-<6 hex>.omni-os.app CNAME per machine, and the server runs the cloudflared on PATH (or OMNI_CLOUDFLARED) with the connector token. The request guard accepts the machine's own Connect hostname and still requires the token or a session there, without the Tailscale login check. The relay's /connect page signs in once with Clerk's device grant; opening a machine has the relay sign a mint request that the machine verifies against the relay key it pinned, answered with a one-time code bound to a key made on the machine's origin, which the browser redeems with a proof of that key for an ordinary device session. Turned down: a hosted cross-origin Web UI, per-machine Google sign-in over bare tunnels, Cloudflare Access, and T3's push, reaping and pinned-download extras. ADR 0009.
2026-11-01 T-UPDATE: Omni runs like T3 Code: the desktop app owns the server, so opening the app starts it and quitting stops it, and there is no LaunchAgent (scripts/install-launchd.sh is gone; the Mac app boots out an old com.omni-os.server agent and renames its plist to .plist.disabled; the server runs caffeinate -i -w itself on a Mac). Updating is one Update button. The server fetches origin's main every 15 minutes and compares it with the commit it booted from (GET /api/update, a feed `update` event). Update waits for running turns, runs scripts/sync-main.sh, rebuilds the Web UI and exits with code 75; the app that started it (OMNI_SUPERVISOR=mac or linux) starts it again, so a server without one shows the notice but no button. The Mac app's own button also rebuilds and relaunches the app when the app is behind. The Conductor reaches it as omni_update.
2026-11-02 T-DESKTOP: The desktop app is one Electron shell on the Web UI, as T3 Code's is: apps/desktop loads apps/web unchanged in framework and reaches the OS through one bridge (window.omniDesktop, typed in packages/shared/desktop-bridge.ts, absent in a browser or on a phone). The server is bundled (apps/server/scripts/bundle.ts) and runs on Electron's own Node with ELECTRON_RUN_AS_NODE=1 and OMNI_SUPERVISOR=desktop from a staged folder that mirrors the repo's layout, so the app needs no checkout and no system Node; it attaches to this install's server or starts one, and restarts it after exit 75. It ships a DMG and zip for Mac arm64, x64 and universal and a Linux AppImage, so apps/linux folds into it. The SwiftUI app is replaced, then deleted once Ben has used the new app daily; native SwiftUI controls, ADR 0002's reason, are what is given up. Spike: Electron 44.10.1 bundles Node 24.22.1, node:sqlite loads under ELECTRON_RUN_AS_NODE, node-pty is N-API with darwin prebuilds; Keychain calls from the Electron-Node child are still to be checked on a Mac. The third surface's QA harness is apps/desktop/scripts/qa.ts (Playwright's Electron driver), which refuses port 4747. Supersedes ADR 0002 and 0006. ADR 0010.
2026-11-09 T-ARTSCREEN: Ben's claude.ai Artifacts list comes from the claude CLI's own Artifact tool: a one-off Haiku run (`claude -p --tools Artifact`, `--setting-sources project`, no session) with a PreToolUse guard that lets only an Artifact `list` through, parsed from the tool results (never the model's reply), with a listing accepted only when its rows match its own header count. Failures (cli_missing, signed_out, tool_unavailable, limit, format_changed, timeout, failed) keep the last good list. The cache is machine-local. Rows are keyed by uuid: the short `claude.ai/artifact/<id>` is the uuid in base58, so both link forms join. Refresh on open after 10 minutes, Refresh always, a Daily (default), 6-hourly or Off schedule; type badges mapped once a day. Rows open outside Omni (new tab, shell.openExternal, Linking.openURL). Turned down: scraping or a session cookie, a thread per refresh, model-written JSON, types every run, offline HTML copies. ADR 0011.
2026-11-14 T-PRD667: Machines sign what they push and only signed changes make a machine act. Each machine keeps an Ed25519 key (private half in its secret store, public half as `signKey` in its synced device record next to `sealKey`); every change carries `signer` and `sig` over entity, id, payload hash and timestamp; the relay binds `machine_id` to the token that pushed. Peers' keys are pinned on first sight and change only when Ben confirms a new key in Settings > Sync. A new machine syncs ordinary data at once, but its action entities are refused and secret sends to it are refused until Ben presses Trust in Settings > Sync (the same confirm-key call). Machines already syncing before the Trust build are not trusted automatically: each needs one Trust press after the update. `remote_command`, `assignment`, `update.ask.*`, `automations.owner` and secret drops apply only with a valid signature; other entities are logged and still applied during rollout. `integrations.syncSignatures` / `OMNI_SYNC_SIGNATURES` sets the mode: `actions` (as above), `strict` (every change needs a valid signature) or `log` (unsigned changes apply, commands included; bad signatures and disallowed paths are still refused). Synced `cwd`, `repo_path` and `base_dir` must sit under home, the temp folder, the repos folder, the brain folder, a channel checkout or Omni's `data/threads` or `data/worktrees`, and outside SSH, GPG and cloud credentials and the rest of Omni's data, signed or not (full lists in docs/user/sync.md). Secret drops seal only to a pinned key. The default mode stays `actions`, so unsigned commands are refused at once and every machine updates together: Run on, Hand off and set_secret from an older build fail until it updates. The relay stays trusted for Connect sessions (ADR 0009) and can no longer forge actions from a trusted machine; a never-seen machine it introduces syncs ordinary data but cannot act until Ben trusts it. Turned down: signing only action entities, relay-side signing, mTLS, trust on first sight for actions, holding a new machine's ordinary data, a `log` default. ADR 0012, issue #667.
2026-11-14 T-PAIRTRUST: A machine paired through Omni Connect is trusted with the first signing key pinned for it (its first signed change or device record that verifies), so its remote commands, assignments, update asks, owner claims and secret drops apply at once, with no Trust click. Pairing is the trust step: the relay only takes pushes from Clerk-signed-in machines on ALLOWED_USERS and binds machine_id to the pushing token. A changed key still needs Ben's Confirm new key, and trust stays bound to the confirmed key; actions signed with the announced new key wait in sync_deferred ("waiting for the new key to be confirmed") and apply after the confirm, while any other key is still refused. GET /api/sync/devices adds `held`, shown as "N actions waiting" on the Signing keys row. The migration trusts every machine with a pinned key and no seen key, unblocking machines updated from 0.1.17. Signature checks, role checks, syncSignatures modes, path checks and sealing to pinned keys are unchanged. Given up: a compromised relay can again introduce a never-seen machine that acts (ADR 0012 had turned this down). Turned down: keeping the Trust click, auto-trusting only pre-0.1.17 machines, auto-trusting changed keys. Supersedes ADR 0012's Trust step. ADR 0013, incident 2026-11-14.
2026-11-15 T-BLOCKCARD: What a finished turn leaves open for Ben (questions it asked, decisions it left to him, blockers only he can clear) is derived, not agent-emitted: a Haiku sidecar beside the follow-up-thread one (apps/server/runner/aux.ts, entrypoint omni-os-open-items) reads the turn's text and answers QUESTION/DECISION/BLOCKER lines or NONE, parsed by packages/shared/open-items.ts (at most 5, each one self-contained line, a decision's options after `|`). It runs for every harness with no prompt, tool or output convention of its own, and leaves the agent's reply untouched. Stored as JSON in threads.open_items: local, not synced, updated_at kept, cleared by any message, like next_title. The card answers inline (one reply with every answer), opens a prefilled new-thread composer per item (never starts one unreviewed) and dismisses per item or all (PATCH dismiss_open_item / open_items: null). Turned down: an agent-emitted block or MCP tool (needs a rule and a parser per harness, and agents forget it), a regex over the reply (misses decisions phrased as statements and flags rhetorical questions), one Haiku call for both cards (couples two answers that fail independently).
2026-11-16 T-OMNICLI-BUILD: Omni has an `omni` command (apps/cli) with six commands and no more: new (with --wait: exit 0 done, 1 failed or stopped, 2 needs you), send, threads/show, status (--machine reads another synced machine from GET /machines and the update fleet), doctor (works with the server down: port probe via /api/auth/proof, token, data dir, disk, claude/codex/gh logins, server log tail) and open (desktop app via --route or omni://, else the browser; --pair prints a pairing link). It is a thin client over /api through @omni/client-core, tested against the wire fixtures. Auth is OMNI_URL plus OMNI_TOKEN or OMNI_TOKEN_FILE, then the install's api-token. The desktop app writes a /bin/sh shim on every launch that runs the bundled CLI on its own binary with ELECTRON_RUN_AS_NODE=1 and -e (the AppImage file on Linux, since its binary lives in a mount), to its bin folder (on every thread's PATH) and to ~/.local/bin/omni; it replaces its own shim and Omni's old launchers, leaves any other file, and only the installed app on its default port, data dir and profile claims ~/.local/bin. A second launch now hands its --route or omni:// link to the running app, and electron-builder registers the omni scheme. Threads without the omni MCP are told about the command when the shim is on PATH. scripts/omni.sh became a checkout wrapper; the dead omarchy launcher goes. The CLI is exempt from every surface: a feature does not grow a command. Turned down: cleanup only, mirroring the 59 MCP tools, a standalone npm or curl install, a shim on the AppImage mount path, an update command, a headless server mode. ADR 0014.
2026-11-17 T-RUSTTWINS: The TypeScript twins of the ask, render and threads MCP servers (apps/mcp/ask.ts, render.ts, threads.ts) and of the omni command (apps/cli) are deleted, after 0.1.30 shipped omni-mcp and omni. The Rust binaries are the only build: the desktop build fails without cargo (and a C compiler for omni) unless --native off, which ships an app whose threads go without the three tools and which writes no omni command. A checkout builds omni-mcp into target/debug (npm run dev, npm start, qa-stack, the server tests) and scripts/omni.sh builds omni the same way; the server leaves the three servers out, with a warning, when the binary does not start. npm test still passes without cargo: the tests that start those servers skip. The recorded scenarios in packages/test-fixtures/native are now the contract the Rust tests hold, edited by hand. The conductor's omni.ts and apps/mcp/external stay TypeScript. Turned down: keeping the TypeScript as a fallback (two implementations to keep in step for a path no release takes), the server running cargo itself at start. Amends ADR 0014.
2026-11-18 T-91A6: Omni Connect registration is owned. A registered machine id stays with the relay token that registered it; another token gets it only with a proof signed by the machine's enrolled key (omni-connect-register+jwt, bound to the user, the bearer token's hash and the key registered), so the same machine signed in again recovers it and nothing transfers. A token bound to one sync machine registers only that id. The relay claims a new id in its Durable Object before any Cloudflare call, names new tunnels omni-<user tag>-<id>-<random hex> recorded before creation, and reuses a tunnel or DNS record only on its record of it; a DNS record it cannot prove is refused with 409 hostname_taken. Legacy rows adopt their recorded omni-<id> tunnel once when its ingress serves their hostname. Turned down: longer hostname suffixes (would change every new hostname) and renaming legacy tunnels. ADR 0009 amendment.
2026-11-19 T-C479: Each spawn of a thread's local harness gets its own token (omt_ + 32 random bytes) in OMNI_TOKEN and in data/threads/<id>/token for OMNI_TOKEN_FILE, never the install token; the server keeps its SHA-256 in the machine-local thread_tokens table (never synced), revokes it on the spawn's exit, the next spawn, server start and thread delete, and takes it only straight from this machine. callerOf decides the calling thread for every guard: the token's thread, else x-omni-thread from an install-token caller (only narrows), else Ben; a token whose header names another thread is a 403. A thread token's messages are the Conductor's, it cannot answer a question card, pair anything, open a terminal, add or run a channel action, approve a learning, set access levels, change queued messages, promote a quick ask to a task, approve an MCP client, finish setup or turn auto-merge on, and a thread it creates files under it (parent outside its lineage: 403). Under an automation or usage scan it also cannot rotate the install token, continue, message, compact, retry or resume, relay page comments to or review-note a thread outside its lineage, or start a PR review; outside one, continue and rotate_install_token keep working. In an agent's thread (OMNI_THREAD_DIR set) the omni command never falls back to data/api-token; it may read the thread folder's token, and Ben's thread terminal and channel actions keep the install token. Turned down: HMAC or signed tokens, a new OMNI_THREAD_TOKEN variable, refusing the header. Part 3 (OS sandbox for unattended threads) is separate. ADR 0016, audit finding 12.
2026-11-19 T-CONNECT-B1: Custom harnesses (slice B1 of crew plan Addendum A). A remote agent the user registers is a file, data/harnesses/<slug>.yaml (name, kind runs-api, url, auth.secret, bills, family, cap, capabilities that only turn off), with id custom:<slug>, run on the Hermes adapter under its own id. Files sync as harness_file, a signed action entity; the secret's value stays in each machine's secret store. The secret a file names and HERMES_API_KEY are server-only: secretReachesThread leaves them out of every thread env, terminal and channel action, env-guard strips HERMES_API_KEY from every harness, and the runner hands the key to that harness's HTTP client only (AdapterContext.harnessKey); a Runs API thread gets no channel or global secret at all. A file cannot name Omni's own credentials or any OMNI_ name; http:// only to loopback or a Tailscale address; redirects never followed. HarnessCapabilities gains remote, toolLimits and switchable, read instead of ids for isProviderHarness, the delegate-only and access-level refusals and switching. adapterFor no longer falls back to Claude Code: a thread on a custom harness with no file here, or an unknown id, refuses before anything local starts, and a live session checks again before each turn. Writes to /api/harnesses/custom need the install token or a token-paired session and no agent thread. Hermes keeps its id, Settings URL and key, with no migration. Turned down: Hermes as custom:hermes (a thread migration for no gain), per-machine harness config, A2A first, per-harness strip lists for user-named keys. ADR 0017.
2026-11-20 T-EVE-CONNECT: eve agents connect as custom harnesses with `kind: eve`. Omni speaks eve's own /eve/v1 API (harness/eve/client.ts behind harness/remote.ts): a thread's first turn creates an eve session whose id the thread keeps as omni-eve-<id>, later turns post to it with turnPolicy queue, a run is one turn read from the stream's tail index, Omni's prompt goes as clientContext, Stop is cancel, steering is off (eve's steer cancels and restarts the turn), and approvals or sign-ins eve asks for are a status line. Find detects eve by GET /eve/v1/health, reads an optional `{ name, description, code_url }` offer from GET /v1/omni/pair's 405, shows Get a code for a code page on the agent's own host, and recognizes Vercel's login wall. Sign-in reuses the pairing protocol, served by the agent at its root; Nomos is the reference (stateless HMAC codes on /omni behind its web login, keys omni_<id>_<hmac>, a revoked list, the key acting as the owner, and a deployment without NOMOS_WEB_PASSWORD refusing to open its chat). Turned down: a Runs API shim in each eve agent, a pairing helper script, signing in with the web password, Vercel OIDC or the protection bypass secret, eve's steer. ADR 0018.
2026-11-20 T-9079 (group threads): A thread is a group chat with agents. Every agent has a name and an avatar (uploaded in Settings > Agents, else a colored initials disc; a remote agent may offer one when it connects; no per-agent voice); messages show the agent's name and avatar, with harness and model only when Show technical details is on. The agent a thread started as is its lead. An agent mention (@agent) in Ben's message seats that agent as a member for good, with its own session in that thread, until Ben removes it from the member list (its replies stay, its session closes); an agent is seated at most once per thread. A message with no mention goes to the lead; a message that names several agents makes each reply in parallel, and the lead stays quiet unless named. Each agent gets every message since its own last turn as context (Ben's, the lead's, other members'), never tool calls or tool output. Agents share the thread's one worktree, run in parallel and keep their own powers; when two touch the same file, Omni tells each running agent which files the other changed this turn and the thread flags every file both edited. Agents never seat agents, except the Conductor, and only in a delegated thread it creates in the client channel, never in #conductor. delegate_team makes one thread with a lead and seated members: the Conductor posts each part as a message naming its member, members run in parallel, and the lead is woken with their replies once all finish and writes the crew report; delegate is unchanged. The status mark shows the busiest agent (Needs you, else Working, else Done) with avatars for who runs. Seating replaces Ask for new messages. Not built yet; plan docs/plans/2026-11-20-group-threads.md. Supersedes the glossary's one-agent Thread, Ask and Team, and threads.md's "Omni never seats a second agent in a thread". ADR 0019.
2026-11-20 T-9079 (remote agents): Connecting Hermes or an eve agent creates an agent with a name and an avatar (its offered one, else initials). It leaves the harness and model pickers; its server (today's custom harness file, key and cap) is a detail shown only in that agent's settings. A remote agent can lead a thread talk-only, and Ben seats a local member to change files; it can also be seated as a member. A per-channel switch, off by default and set only by Ben, gives remote agents in that channel's threads a read tool scoped to the thread's worktree (list, read, search; no writes, nothing outside the worktree); it reuses or extends "No thread excerpts to remote agents". Keys stay server-only and remote agents still get no secrets, no install token and no Omni tools besides that read tool. Not built yet; plan slices 4 and 5. Supersedes ADR 0017's E1 naming and "a remote agent gets nothing else" (for the read tool only), and ADR 0018's "eve agents connect as custom harnesses". ADR 0020.
2026-11-20 T-52F8 (group threads UI): Ben picked the group threads UI from a throwaway prototype (branch prototype/group-threads, never merged). Replies show the agent's avatar in a 24px gutter with its name and time beside it. One @ menu, agents first (members, then agents a mention would seat), then machines and files, plus a hint line under the composer naming who gets the message. The members' avatars in the thread header open the member list (popover on desktop, sheet on the phone, Remove per member); the quick-actions card keeps Lead and Members rows. Parallel turns split into a lane per agent with the collision flag above, one lane per tab on the phone. In the desktop thread list the avatars are the mark while agents run or wait on Ben: the one asking first with the Needs you ring and diamond, working ones after in the Working ring, two at most, else one and a count; phone rows say it on their second line. A remote lead shows quiet labels ("talks only" in the crumbs and composer, a lock line under a reply that could not read files, a Files row in the quick-actions card). Settings > Agents opens an agent as a page with Avatar and, for a remote agent, Server and In threads cards. Turned down without recorded reasons: byline and card replies, a To line, a members strip, inline and docked collisions, mark plus avatars, a notice card, the editor modal. ADR 0019 and ADR 0020 (How it looks), plan section 3.5 and Q48 to Q58.
2026-11-20 T-52F8 (group threads answers): Ben answered 21 of the plan's open questions. A newly seated member's first turn gets a catch-up summary written by the lead, then everything since its last turn. The lead is fixed once the thread starts; to change it, start a new thread. Stop stops every running agent, with no per-agent Stop. Only the lead commits and opens the PR; members edit files only. Hermes and custom:* threads convert to agent-led threads with that remote agent as lead and stay usable. Cursor Cloud can be a member, on its own branch, its changes coming back as a separate diff. A team with two parts for one agent seats it once and the Conductor gives it both parts in one message. Avatars sync with the agent's crew file to every machine, phone included. Status ranking: Needs you, Failed, Working, Done, Idle. One per-channel switch, "Remote agents can see this channel's work", covers thread excerpts and worktree reads, off by default, replacing "No thread excerpts to remote agents". The remote read tool sees git-tracked files only. Changes shows one combined diff with no per-agent credit; collision flags are enough. UI: initials discs use two unique letters (Researcher RS, Reviewer RV), color only below 18px; a quiet "Designer joined" line on seating; lanes stay as lanes in the history; the composer's model picker changes only the lead's model; the "Goes to X only" hint shows once an @ is typed, and with no @ the placeholder names the lead; agent pills carry the agent's avatar in the accent color, machine pills a device icon in the current blue; collision flags show folder and file (thread/Composer.tsx) with the full path on hover; Connect moves into Settings > Agents as an "Add remote agent" button next to the agent list. Still open: the header avatars repeating the Working label (Q54) and the quick-actions card of a remote-led thread (Q57); Q59 to Q68 hold the parts the answers left open or raised. ADR 0019, ADR 0020, plan section 5, GLOSSARY (Lead, Member, Seat, Catch-up, Status mark, Remote agent, Team).
2026-11-20 T-52F8 (group threads answers, round 2): Ben answered Q54, Q57 and Q59 to Q68. The catch-up is written by Omni with a small model, not by the lead (replaces the earlier Q6 answer). A thread whose lead agent is hidden or deleted goes read-only; start a new thread. In a remote-led thread, local members edit and Omni shows Ben a Commit button; Ben commits. When a Cursor Cloud member finishes, the lead pulls its branch into the thread's branch and resolves conflicts. Usage limit, Setup failed and Message held rank with Failed; Stopped ranks with Done. One agent failed while another works: the face mark shows the working face plus a red dot on the row, so Failed outranks Working in order only, not in the mark. Three or more agents running: lanes stack vertically, each collapsed to its latest line. A local agent bound to a remote server becomes a remote agent with the same name and avatar. The remote read tool is text only with a 1 MB cap: binary refused, larger text truncated with a note. Removing a member writes a quiet "Designer left" line. The thread header shows only the avatars, running ones ringed, and drops the Working label. A remote-led thread's details panel has a local thread's rows, empty where they do not apply. Agent pills in each agent's own color, confirmed. "Remote agents can see this channel's work" starts on in channels that send excerpts to remote agents today and off everywhere else. Raised: Q69, which channels count as sending excerpts, since they flow by default. ADR 0019, ADR 0020, plan section 5, GLOSSARY (Lead, Catch-up, Status mark).
2026-11-20 T-52F8 (group threads Q69): "Remote agents can see this channel's work" starts on only in channels where an Ask actually sent an excerpt to a remote agent (an ask event with remote set and an excerpt). Every other channel starts off, new channels included, even though excerpts flow by default today. ADR 0020, plan Q69 and slice 5.
2026-11-20 T-4092 (group threads answers, round 4): Ben answered the plan's last open questions. Avatars: icon and emoji on a color stay as an option beside upload and the initials default; built-in agents keep their marks (doc reading, Ben did not name them). Agent names are unique ignoring case, checked on save. Add an agent in the member list seats with a joined line and no turn, replacing Ask an agent. Ben seats agents in any thread, #conductor threads and quick asks included (his first answer was no; he changed it the same day); a member in a quick ask gets read-only tools; the Conductor still seats only in threads it delegates, never in #conductor. Any agent except the Conductor can be seated; one the machine cannot run fails when it runs. The @ menu has tabs Machines | Agents | Files, opening on Agents; artifacts sit in Files under their own heading. Removing a member mid-turn lets the turn finish, drops its queued messages, and the left line follows. Handoff, Take over, fork and continue are refused until every agent is idle, then every session moves together. A member runs on its agent's defaults, then the channel's, then Auto, switchable per member from the member list. The crew report goes when the lead's turn ends, even with members running; a team thread sends it after the lead's turn that follows every member finishing. Cursor Agent hears of collisions at its next turn. A collision flag clears at the lead's next commit (Ben's Commit in a remote-led thread) or when Ben dismisses it. One checkpoint per message across all agents; undo restores the state from before that message. Running avatars show on Inbox cards, phone Activity and the Overview dashboard too, two at most, then one plus a count. Hermes keeps the id hermes. A remote agent gets a charter only if Ben wrote one in its file, else the thread framing and its server's own instructions. With technical details off, a remote agent's page shows connection status, Test connection and Remove. A remote-led thread gets its worktree at the start. A remote agent's own tools on its own server do not change. Auto never picks a remote agent. With the channel switch off, a remote agent can still be seated or lead but gets only the message addressed to it. The read tool also returns the thread's diff and changed-file list, leaving out untracked and ignored files. A team lead runs a planning turn, then messages members through a tool only a team lead gets; @name in replies stays plain text; the lead gives a two-part agent both parts. The lead waits while a member waits on Ben; a failed or stopped member counts as finished; no time limit. The Conductor can seat agents with mentions in follow-ups to threads it created, and delegate takes an optional member list. iOS stays slice 7. Builder defaults Ben did not pick: Q3 PNG/JPEG/WebP up to 2 MB, square crop, 256px, inline in the crew file; Q7 carryover budget with the oldest messages summarized; Q16 no thread cap, one slot per agent in its harness's cap; Q18 follow-up and Open items read the lead's reply, else the last to finish, title unchanged; Q19 an older client's Ask becomes a seat; Q29 offered avatar as image data up to 256 KB, reread on reconnect unless Ben set one; Q38 read tool carried through the protocol as a client-side tool call; Q39 per-turn in-memory token keyed to thread, turn and worktree, revoked at turn end and restart; Q46 one task id per team thread, crew_prs reports its one PR, crew_questions names the asking member. ADR 0019, ADR 0020, plan section 5 (with Where answers met earlier decisions), GLOSSARY (Seat, Team, Crew report, Collision, Quick ask).
